<section><div class="wrap feature-lead"><div class="feature-copy"><span class="section-tag">The reading, explained</span><h2>Charge and health answer different questions.</h2><p>Charge tells you how full the battery is now. Health estimates how much energy it can hold compared with its original design capacity. WhatBattery calculates that figure from the capacity reported by the battery and shows one decimal place.</p><p>The capacity is an estimate from the battery’s own gauge. It can move as the gauge recalibrates, so a small change alone does not establish faster wear or a need for replacement. Cycle count and Apple’s Normal or Service Recommended condition provide more context.</p><p>When macOS’s health figure differs, WhatBattery shows both. Pro adds a longer record so you can follow the trend.</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>Health computed from nominal capacity ÷ design capacity</li><li>Service condition reported by macOS</li><li>macOS’s health figure shown alongside when it differs</li><li>Health, cycles and condition are free; raw mAh capacities are Pro</li></ul></div><img src="/img/screenshot-mac.png" alt="WhatBattery Mac overview showing battery health, capacity, cycle count, temperature, and service condition" width="440" /></div></section>

<section class="soft-section"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="section-tag">The honest boundary</span><h2>It tells you what the battery reports and what cannot be known.</h2><p>There is no dependable public signal that proves a battery is genuine. WhatBattery does not add a green “genuine” badge to incomplete evidence. It shows the service condition, capacities, health, cycles, and identifiers the system can actually support.</p></div><div class="card-grid"><article class="card"><h3>Matches macOS</h3><p>The condition is read from the system, so a service warning agrees with System Settings.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Does not control charging</h3><p>WhatBattery observes. It never enforces a charge limit or writes to the battery.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Keeps missing data missing</h3><p>If the system refuses a reading, the app shows that it is unavailable instead of guessing.</p></article></div></div></section>

